WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.950
Our class asked: does

2
00:00:01.950 --> 00:00:03.130
sleep help our marks?

3
00:00:03.820 --> 00:00:05.940
We collected sleep hours and test

4
00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.600
marks for everyone in the class.

5
00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:09.880
Two numbers for each of us.

6
00:00:10.490 --> 00:00:12.230
How could we see if they go together?

7
00:00:13.550 --> 00:00:17.170
Today: scatter plots, correlation, and why

8
00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:19.710
together is not the same as cause.

9
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:22.610
A scatter plot puts one

10
00:00:22.610 --> 00:00:25.010
measurement across and the other up.

11
00:00:25.620 --> 00:00:27.740
Each pupil becomes one dot.

12
00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:30.950
Sleep goes along the bottom, and

13
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.330
the mark goes up the side.

14
00:00:33.560 --> 00:00:35.620
If the dots rise from left to right,

15
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.400
that is a positive relationship.

16
00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.700
If they fall, it is negative, like

17
00:00:40.700 --> 00:00:42.860
screen time late at night and marks.

18
00:00:43.590 --> 00:00:45.250
If they make a shapeless cloud,

19
00:00:45.250 --> 00:00:47.030
there is no relationship.

20
00:00:48.340 --> 00:00:51.460
The correlation, called r, measures how

21
00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:53.600
close the dots are to a straight line.

22
00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.740
It goes from minus one to one.

23
00:00:57.370 --> 00:00:59.970
Near one is a strong positive pattern,

24
00:01:00.390 --> 00:01:02.690
near zero is no straight-line pattern.

25
00:01:03.910 --> 00:01:05.730
Here is a famous pattern.

26
00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.000
Ice cream sales and

27
00:01:08.000 --> 00:01:09.840
drownings rise together.

28
00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:12.620
Does ice cream make people drown?

29
00:01:13.310 --> 00:01:13.990
No.

30
00:01:15.020 --> 00:01:16.880
Hot weather brings both.

31
00:01:18.290 --> 00:01:21.130
So when sleep and marks go together,

32
00:01:21.590 --> 00:01:23.450
ask what else could explain it.

33
00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:26.500
Families with calm homes may give

34
00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:29.100
children both more sleep and more

35
00:01:29.100 --> 00:01:29.940
help with homework.

36
00:01:31.330 --> 00:01:31.730
Quick check!

37
00:01:32.350 --> 00:01:34.710
The dots lie very close to a

38
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:35.930
rising straight line.

39
00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:37.710
Which r fits best?

40
00:01:39.060 --> 00:01:40.440
About zero point nine.

41
00:01:41.110 --> 00:01:43.630
Rising means positive, and close

42
00:01:43.630 --> 00:01:45.190
to a line means strong.

43
00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.260
Minus zero point nine would be a

44
00:01:48.260 --> 00:01:50.080
strong falling pattern.

45
00:01:51.340 --> 00:01:53.420
In the app, open Scatter,

46
00:01:53.860 --> 00:01:55.480
correlation and best fit.

47
00:01:56.080 --> 00:01:58.640
Tap to add pupils who study a lot but

48
00:01:58.640 --> 00:02:01.180
score low, and watch r fall.

49
00:02:02.450 --> 00:02:02.990
Your turn.

50
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:06.280
Pupils with more books at home get higher

51
00:02:06.280 --> 00:02:09.240
marks, with r of zero point six.

52
00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.550
Does that prove books cause higher marks?

53
00:02:13.900 --> 00:02:15.400
No, not on its own.

54
00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:18.390
Remember: a scatter plot shows two

55
00:02:18.390 --> 00:02:21.350
measurements, r measures how close they

56
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:24.670
are to a line, and correlation is not

57
00:02:24.670 --> 00:02:25.170
cause.

58
00:02:25.920 --> 00:02:28.700
Always ask what else could explain it.

59
00:02:29.890 --> 00:02:31.870
Try it yourself: measure the

60
00:02:31.870 --> 00:02:33.810
height and arm span of five

61
00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:35.650
people at home, and plot them.

62
00:02:36.240 --> 00:02:38.420
Is there a pattern, and does one

63
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:39.840
really cause the other?
